</ul><h1>Cute, Functional</h1><p>The other day, I had to run some errands and pick up a few essential items for the family, which meant I ended up at the big box store that has the bullseye as a logo. But the store itself isn’t important here, nor are the essential items I purchased.</p>
<p>What sticks with me from this trip is what happened as I left. Passing the customer service desk, en route to the exit, I heard the person working there say to a coworker, “Well, I was born in 2009, so–”</p>
<p>And then, presumably, they said some other words to finish the sentence, but unfortunately I didn’t hear them because they were drowned out by a voice in my head screaming, over and over again, “Oh my god, you are so old!”</p>
<p>Like, this was a person WITH A JOB. And they were born after I had FINISHED college.</p>
<p>UGH!</p>
<p>But this random retail employee is not the 2009-born entity I want to discuss today. No, in relation to these pillows, I want to talk about another thing that came out of 2009: <em>Minecraft</em>.</p>
<p>Look, I know everyone’s obsessed with a different video game right now. But OMG, can you believe that level of staying power? It’s almost two decades old and still going strong! And its continued existence isn’t just some sort of niche thing, either. You constantly see new <em>Minecraft</em> recruits out in the world, kids as young as five and six-year-olds who love to… mine… and build stuff?</p>
<p>I should admit here that I’ve never played the game.</p>
<p>Now, I should note that there is no official tie between this product we’re offering today and the <em>Minecraft</em> empire. And yet it would be ridiculous to claim that <em>Minecraft</em>, by making cube-y design cool, isn’t at least somewhat responsible for these things.</p>
<p>But even if your kiddo isn’t a <em>Minecraft</em> fan, these are still great potential gifts for two reasons:</p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>They’ll be a surprise. Because all wrapped up under the tree, they’ll just look like a box, so your child won’t have any idea they’re about to open up something cute and fun; and…</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>They turn something essential and boring into a fun toy, which is all a kid wants. Seriously, a pillow? That you can play with?? How awesome is that?!</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>Wow, this one got pretty ramble-y. Sorry. I’m still recovering from the realization that someone born in 2009 is old enough to work.</p>